DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These routines return the character, of type cchhttyyppee, at the current po‐
       sition in the named window.  If any attributes are set for  that  posi‐
       tion,  their  values  are OR'ed into the value returned.  Constants de‐
       fined in <<ccuurrsseess..hh>> can be used with the && (logical  AND)  operator  to
       extract the character or attributes alone.

   AAttttrriibbuutteess
       The  following  bit  masks  may  be  AND-ed with characters returned by
       wwiinncchh.

       Lb Lb Lb Lx.  Name Description _ A_CHARTEXT     Extract character A_AT‐
       TRIBUTES   Extract attributes A_COLOR   Extract color pair information

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       Functions  prefixed with “mv” first perform cursor movement and fail if
       the position (_y, _x) is outside the window boundaries.

       The wwiinncchh function does not return an  error  if  the  window  contains
       characters  larger than 8-bits (255).  Only the low-order 8 bits of the
       character are used by wwiinncchh.

NNOOTTEESS
       Note that all of these routines may be macros.

PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       These functions are described in X/Open Curses, Issue 4.

       Very old systems (before standardization) provide a different  function
       with the same name:

       •   The  wwiinncchh  function  was  part of the original BSD curses library,
           which stored a 7-bit character combined with  the  _s_t_a_n_d_o_u_t  attri‐
           bute.

           In  BSD  curses,  wwiinncchh returned only the character (as an integer)
           with the _s_t_a_n_d_o_u_t attribute removed.

       •   System V curses added support for several  video  attributes  which
           could be combined with characters in the window.

           Reflecting this improvement, the function was altered to return the
           character combined with all video attributes in a cchhttyyppee value.

       X/Open Curses does not specify the size and layout of attributes, color
       and  character  values in cchhttyyppee; it is implementation-dependent.  This
       implementation uses 8 bits for character values.  An application  using
       more bits, e.g., a Unicode value, should use the wide-character equiva‐
       lents to these functions.

